\input{sections/tab_main}

We ask whether the guarantee holds (\S\ref{sec:main}), what a tolerance buys (\S\ref{sec:tradeoff}), what
limits the savings (\S\ref{sec:monotone}), how the score and the reference matter (\S\ref{sec:ablations}), and which ingredients make certification efficient
(\S\ref{sec:testchoice}).

\subsection{The guarantee holds}
\label{sec:main}

Proposition~\ref{prop:risk} bounds the probability, over the calibration sample, that the deployed
policy loses more than $\eps$. We check this event directly by treating each recorded pool as the
task distribution, drawing calibration sets i.i.d.\ from it and computing the deployed policy's
exact risk: over 5{,}000 draws per setting and tolerance the failure rate is at most 3.4\% (upper
95\% limit 3.9\%) against $\delta = 10\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:checks}). The finite-pool violation rate
of the replay agrees: at most 2\% in Table~\ref{tab:main} and 3\% anywhere in the tolerance sweep.

\input{sections/tab_baselines}

The guarantee is what distinguishes these savings from those of simple rules (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).
Their losses can be modest on average but are large on some settings, with nothing at deployment to say
which: always stopping at $L_0$ loses up to 20.6 points, always stopping at $L_1$ up to 9.4, and a
threshold on $L_0$'s confidence that spends as much as \climb{} on average up to 15.9; three of the four settings where
it loses over 5 points are those where Gemma's confidence is inverted (Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}d). \climb{}'s
mean loss stays within its tolerance on every setting: at most 2.8 points at $\eps = 5\%$ and 7.2 at 10\%. At a similar cost,
always stopping at $L_1$ loses less on average than \climb{} at $\eps = 10\%$ (3.3 against 5.0 points),
but nothing bounds its loss on a given setting; at $\eps = 5\%$ \climb{}'s mean and largest losses are both
below $L_1$'s, though it spends 62\% of tokens to $L_1$'s 43\%.

\subsection{What a tolerance buys}
\label{sec:tradeoff}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_tradeoff.pdf}
\caption{(a) Tokens as the tolerance $\eps$ varies (100 splits per point; violation rates at most
3\%). (b) Tokens as the calibration set grows ($\eps = 5\%$ and $10\%$) on four representative settings.}
\label{fig:tradeoff}
\end{figure}

The team improves on the direct answer on every setting, by 2.0 to 20.6 points, so savings
are paid for in accuracy, and $\eps$ sets the price. At $\eps = 2\%$ \climb{} saves little, at most 16\%
of the pipeline's tokens on gpt-oss-20b MuSiQue and nothing on MATH (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:tradeoff}a). At 5\% it spends 31 to
96\% of the pipeline's tokens, 62\% on average, for losses of at most 2.8 points; at 10\% it spends 18 to
62\%, 35\% on average. Savings are smallest where the direct answer's confidence is inverted (Gemma on FRAMES and BBEH), where the certified policy largely falls back to the team, and on FRAMES, whose long documents the cheap rungs also pay for.

Savings also need data. With 50 calibration tasks \climb{} certifies almost nothing, with 100 only at
10\%, and savings at 5\% need a few hundred (Figure~\ref{fig:tradeoff}b). Recording them costs 1.5M to
79M tokens, repaid after 340 to 2{,}000 queries at $\eps = 10\%$ and 460 to 4{,}400 at 5\%, except on
Gemma's BBEH, where the small saving at 5\% takes 13{,}000
(Appendix~\ref{app:calibcost}).

\subsection{What limits the savings}
\label{sec:monotone}

Two things largely decide how much \climb{} can save, and neither affects the guarantee. \emph{The signal.} By
Proposition~\ref{prop:threshold} a threshold is optimal when the gain from escalating falls with
confidence. On the nine settings where $L_0$'s confidence ranks answers correctly (AUROC 0.63 to 0.81) the
gain falls on eight: pooled over them, the gain falls from 21.6 points for the least confident tenth of tasks to
4 to 7 points in each of the five most confident tenths (Figure~\ref{fig:criterion} in Appendix~\ref{app:monotone}). On Gemma's FRAMES, MuSiQue and BBEH the
ranking is inverted (AUROC 0.22 to 0.34): greedy decoding without thinking falls into repetitions with
high log-probability, and the certified policy relies on agreement or the team.
\emph{The design.} Stopping on the
agreement of the two cheap rungs is cheaper where the direct answer is weak, a threshold on $L_0$ where it
is strong; choosing between them on calibration data, as \climb{} does, is among the cheapest of five
designs certified the same way, and a learned estimate of the signed gain does no better on average
(Appendix~\ref{sec:variants}). An oracle stopping at the cheapest correct rung beats the pipeline by up to 12 points, so much of the headroom lies in better signals.

\subsection{Which score and which reference}
\label{sec:ablations}

\input{sections/tab_ablations_main}

Table~\ref{tab:ablmain} changes one ingredient at a time. \emph{The score.} Every score keeps the
guarantee, a random one included, but the score decides the savings. On gpt-oss, log-probability and
agreement together save the most. On Gemma, whose direct answers are confidently wrong on three settings,
adding agreement to log-probability spends less (71 against 82\% at $\eps = 5\%$), and a random score less
still (66\%). Where log-probability misleads, the certified policy falls back to the team, so a misleading
score tends to cost savings rather than accuracy. At $\eps = 10\%$, which exceeds the team's net gain over $L_0$ on most
settings, even random stopping is affordable; the signal matters most at tight tolerances (57 against 67\%
on gpt-oss at 5\%). \emph{The
reference.} The guarantee holds against any reference, but the cheaper it is and the closer to the cheap
rungs, the less there is to save: at $\eps = 5\%$, \climb{} spends 73\% of the reference's tokens with the team's first draft as reference and 89\% with $L_1$ (Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}).

\subsection{What makes certification efficient}
\label{sec:testchoice}

\input{sections/tab_calib}

Table~\ref{tab:calib} swaps parts of the procedure, keeping it valid. \emph{Adapting to the variance matters.} On most stopped tasks the cheap answer and the reference are both right or both wrong, so most losses are zero; mapped to $[0, 1]$
they sit at $1/2$, and the Hoeffding--Bentkus bound
of \citet{wynn2026corrupted}, which assumes the worst variance at that mean, certifies nothing cheaper than the pipeline.
\emph{Keeping the sign also matters.} Clipping it to the harm indicator counts the answers stopping loses but
not those it saves, and certifies much less where the team often loses correct answers, for instance 83
against 44\% of the pipeline's tokens on gpt-oss-20b MuSiQue (Table~\ref{tab:calibfull}); an exact binomial
test of the clipped loss is cheapest on only three settings, two of them on MATH, where the team rarely loses
correct answers. A Bonferroni correction in place of sequential testing loses most of the rest.
